76
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,840
This week. I want to talk about something that arose out of one of our live chat

77
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:10,960
sessions.

78
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:13,760
I'm going to do that in Scout MasterChip in seven minutes or less,

79
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:16,400
and that's going to take up the remainder of the podcast.

80
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:18,400
So let's get started, shall we?

81
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:48,400
Scout MasterChip in seven minutes or less?

82
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:57,280
This past Wednesday on the chat had a really useful discussion

83
00:05:57,920 --> 00:06:05,520
about being a Scouter who is not in a key position within a troop- or any unit

84
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:10,880
really- but feel that those who are in key positions might be misapplying

85
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:14,960
scouting or may not understand scouting the same way that they do

86
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:20,000
Now. I think it's reasonable to agree that there are people in key positions

87
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:22,640
in scouting who don't have a handle on some of the basics

88
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:26,320
and most of the time the basics they're missing have to do with engaging

89
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:29,919
youth members in leadership and the patrol system.

90
00:06:29,919 --> 00:06:34,560
A number of people who contact me do so because they're kind of

91
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:38,640
dissatisfied and they want to do something about the way these things are handled

92
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:41,120
in their unit, but they're not in a key role to do so

93
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:45,120
and they ask my advice on how to influence change.

94
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:50,480
And at the same time, I also hear from Scouters who feel kind of underutilized

95
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:53,360
that there's not much for them to do because there are a lot of other

96
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:54,800
Scouters involved.

97
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:56,480
They're just trying to find a way to help out.

98
00:06:56,480 --> 00:06:59,520
They have lots of energy- very positive-

99
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:01,920
and they feel like they've really got a Tiger by the tail.

100
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:03,760
They understand what they're up to

101
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:07,920
and they really really want to help, but they don't see a whole lot for them to do.

102
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:12,640
Let's start with the idea that may solve some of these problems

103
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:16,480
okay, and that is that scouting was not really designed to have

104
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:19,039
a high ratio of adults involved.

105
00:07:19,039 --> 00:07:22,159
Now, I know when I say something like that it's going to raise hackles,

106
00:07:22,159 --> 00:07:27,599
but honestly, if we have scouting in the way that it was designed

107
00:07:27,599 --> 00:07:31,120
and the way that it really works well, in my opinion

108
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:35,920
there's not a whole lot of adult involvement because Scouts are doing

109
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:40,800
what Scouts do and adults have a pretty minor role in that.

110
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:45,520
Now we do talk a lot about the ratio of adults to Scouts when we're dealing

111
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:49,920
with situations that are safety-oriented, and that's very wise.

112
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:53,600
Sometimes you need a higher ratio of adults to Scouts in those situations.

113
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:58,080
but I think what we miss a lot of times is the way that that ratio

114
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:01,520
affects the delivery of the program and the experience of our Scouts.

115
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:04,880
The idea as long as I've been around is the more the better.

116
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:11,760
but obviously there's got to be a point where the ratio of adults to Scouts

117
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:17,280
kind of starts to break things down a bit. and the other idea I think we have to accept

118
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:20,159
is that everybody who wants to be in a key position,

119
00:08:20,799 --> 00:08:23,760
who wants to be the Scout Master or Committee Chair,

120
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:26,000
is not going to be offered either position,

121
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:28,560
and that could be frustrating.

122
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:33,200
And furthermore, we're going to have to accept the fact that people in the key positions

123
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:36,880
don't always share your understanding of Scouting.

124
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:39,440
They don't always share my understanding of Scouting.

125
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:45,600
They could be, on analysis, objectively wrong in the way that they do some things,

126
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:50,480
but it's rare that I hear a case where people are volunteering for Scouting

127
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:53,040
and they actually do harm.

128
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:57,280
So we really need to understand that there's no one authentic way of doing things,

129
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:02,319
that or even that you or I have everything right. right,

130
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:05,040
I mean, troops are like families.

131
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:06,400
They have different ways of functioning,

132
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:08,719
They have their own quirks and traditions

133
00:09:08,719 --> 00:09:11,520
And I think as long as Scouts do no harm,

134
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:14,079
our Scouts can get a lot out of the experience.

135
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:18,719
You know, I'll trot out my tired old basketball analogy again. right,

136
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:21,199
You know everybody plays the same game.

137
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:25,680
They play according to the same rules, but everybody plays the game

138
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:27,920
a little bit differently, have different strategies,

139
00:09:27,920 --> 00:09:29,600
different ways they're going to go about doing it.

140
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:32,720
And as long as it's not harming the children who are playing the game,

141
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:36,640
you know who's really going to argue with exactly

142
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:41,440
how things are being done or what's authentic or not authentic.

143
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:43,280
So, accepting those things: first of all,

144
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:48,640
there's a ratio of Scouts to adults that works,

145
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:51,120
and going too far above that or below that,

146
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:54,319
it's going to be detrimental to the program

147
00:09:54,319 --> 00:09:56,480
and to the experience that our Scouts have.

148
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:58,480
We accept the idea that not everybody

149
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:01,280
who wants to have a key role is going to have a key role.

150
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:05,120
We accept the idea that people who are in key roles

151
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:08,880
may not share your particular understanding

152
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:11,760
or mine, of how to do things,

153
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:15,439
But we understand that, if they're not doing harm to Scouts,

154
00:10:16,080 --> 00:10:19,440
that Scouts can still get a lot out of the experience.

155
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:22,240
What do you do if you want to be an active Scouter?

156
00:10:22,240 --> 00:10:23,600
you're not in a key role

157
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:26,400
and there doesn't really seem to be a whole lot for you to do.

158
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:28,640
Well, my best advice is going to be this:

159
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:31,680
First, you start out by observing,

160
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:36,720
with the idea that you want to look for the positives

161
00:10:36,720 --> 00:10:38,320
in the program that you're observing.

162
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:41,600
You want to see what Scouts are getting out of it that's positive.

163
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:42,560
Now, at the same time,

164
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:45,680
you'll be observing things that you really wish you could change,

165
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:49,920
and maybe you'll get the opportunity to be an agent for those changes.

166
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:51,680
but you're not really going to do that

167
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:56,560
until you have a relationship of mutual trust and respect

168
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:59,120
for the people who are in the key positions.

169
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:00,640
These thoughts would apply, too,

170
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:03,680
if you are a Senior Patrol Leader

171
00:11:04,480 --> 00:11:07,440
and you really want to change a lot of things

172
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:08,720
and make things happen.

173
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:11,360
Well, you're going to meet some challenges

174
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:12,960
and you're going to meet some resistance,

175
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:17,520
because those people in key decision-making positions

176
00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:21,040
within your unit need to trust you.

177
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:24,400
They need to form a relationship of trust

178
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:26,000
and mutual respect with you,

179
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:28,800
And the way that you do that- of course,

180
00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:30,000
if it's going to be mutual-

181
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,160
is you show a lot of trust and respect.

182
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:34,640
The wrong way to go about this

183
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:36,320
is probably easier to explain

184
00:11:36,320 --> 00:11:38,240
than the right way to go about it.

185
00:11:38,240 --> 00:11:40,080
So let's say you're sitting at home one evening

186
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:41,760
and I come to the door and knock on the door

187
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:44,480
and I say, hey, I'm here to repaint your living room.

188
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:48,320
And that sounds a little crazy.

189
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:49,760
You didn't really expect anybody

190
00:11:49,760 --> 00:11:51,680
to want to repaint your living room

191
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:53,840
and you really don't know who I am.

192
00:11:53,840 --> 00:11:56,960
And I say, look, look, I'm a very experienced painter.

193
00:11:56,960 --> 00:11:59,440
I have an excellent sense for colors.

194
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:00,880
I've looked at your living room here

195
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:02,400
and I'm going to repaint it

196
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:04,640
to the best color it possibly could be.

197
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:06,400
You're going to look at me and you're going to say,

198
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:08,240
well, you know, we're pretty happy

199
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:09,360
with the way things are.

200
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:12,560
We really like this color.

201
00:12:12,560 --> 00:12:14,320
It took us a long time to figure it out.

202
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:16,960
We'd really rather that you didn't do that.

203
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:19,840
And I could feel very put out at that point

204
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:21,200
because, hey, I went to the paint.

205
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:22,800
I did a lot of work here.

206
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:25,760
I went and I analyzed your color scheme

207
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:27,760
and I went to the paint store and I bought paint

208
00:12:27,760 --> 00:12:29,120
and I got the tools together

209
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:31,360
and I am standing there at your door

210
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:32,880
ready to give you this wonderful gift

211
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:34,640
that you never asked for

212
00:12:34,640 --> 00:12:36,800
and you really wouldn't care to have.

213
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:38,800
And I could get very angry about that,

214
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:40,560
I could be very frustrated, couldn't I?

215
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:43,040
Well, I think it's pretty easy

216
00:12:43,040 --> 00:12:44,240
to see what I'm talking about.

217
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:47,680
If you go into a unit and you say, hey,

218
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:49,360
I've got lots of great ideas

219
00:12:49,360 --> 00:12:51,360
and I really understand scouting really well,

220
00:12:51,360 --> 00:12:53,440
and I listen to this guy on the internet

221
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:55,120
and I read a bunch of his stuff

222
00:12:55,120 --> 00:12:56,960
and I'm just really pumped and excited

223
00:12:56,960 --> 00:12:58,720
and energized about coming in

224
00:12:58,720 --> 00:13:00,240
and changing everything around

225
00:13:00,240 --> 00:13:02,400
to really make things hum and buzz

226
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:03,200
and get going.

227
00:13:03,760 --> 00:13:05,520
Well, you can imagine the kind of reception

228
00:13:05,520 --> 00:13:06,880
that you're going to get right.

229
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:08,560
And this happens if you're an adult

230
00:13:08,560 --> 00:13:10,400
or you're a scout, you know,

231
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:12,400
with a position of responsibility.

232
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:14,720
If you come in gangbusters like that,

233
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:16,080
you're trying to skip over

234
00:13:16,080 --> 00:13:17,760
a very important part of the process,

235
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:20,080
which is a formation of mutual trust and respect.

236
00:13:20,640 --> 00:13:22,640
If I came over to your house

237
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:24,000
and we were having a dinner party

238
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:25,200
and I looked around your living room

239
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:28,000
and I said so this is a really nice color

240
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:29,200
that you've chosen here

241
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:30,560
and it really works well.

242
00:13:30,560 --> 00:13:32,720
If ever you wanted, by the way,

243
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:35,360
to repaint, I want to tell you

244
00:13:35,360 --> 00:13:37,280
I'm a pretty experienced painter

245
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:40,000
and I'd love to do a color analysis

246
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,720
and just show you what I could do

247
00:13:43,280 --> 00:13:45,520
And you know. then you may think,

248
00:13:45,520 --> 00:13:47,360
hey, maybe that's a great idea.

249
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:49,280
Or you could equally say:

250
00:13:49,280 --> 00:13:51,680
you know what we really kind of like

251
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:52,720
the way that it is now.

252
00:13:52,720 --> 00:13:54,400
Do you think this is wrong?

253
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:55,360
And I'd say, well,

254
00:13:55,360 --> 00:13:57,199
I think it'd be interesting to see

255
00:13:57,199 --> 00:13:59,280
what my analysis came up with.

256
00:13:59,280 --> 00:14:01,040
but if you're happy with what you have,

257
00:14:01,600 --> 00:14:02,880
you know I don't want to be pushy,

258
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:04,720
I don't want to push this off on you.

259
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:06,800
I mean, I think that analogy holds up right.

260
00:14:07,280 --> 00:14:09,120
You can't go in like gangbusters

261
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:11,360
and just demand that you're going to change everything

262
00:14:11,360 --> 00:14:12,560
or that you know the right

263
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:14,560
and authentic way of doing things.

264
00:14:14,560 --> 00:14:16,640
How about observing for a while,

265
00:14:16,640 --> 00:14:18,319
Showing your value

266
00:14:18,319 --> 00:14:20,480
and demonstrating how valuable you are

267
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:23,199
by creating that mutual trust and respect?

268
00:14:24,079 --> 00:14:26,160
You know it's pretty easy, right?

269
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:27,520
And when it all comes down to it,

270
00:14:27,520 --> 00:14:29,520
we're looking at the Scout Oath and Law,

271
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:32,319
looking at being friendly and courteous and kind.

272
00:14:32,319 --> 00:14:34,800
We're looking at being obedient and loyal

273
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:36,480
and building a relationship

274
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:38,640
where somebody will probably turn to you

275
00:14:38,640 --> 00:14:40,000
and say: look, what do you think?

276
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,040
How do you think we're doing?

277
00:14:41,680 --> 00:14:44,000
What would you suggest that we do differently?

278
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:45,760
That's entirely possible.

279
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:49,280
It might not be really likely all the time,

280
00:14:49,280 --> 00:14:50,800
but that's entirely possible.

281
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:53,280
But if you go into a situation like this

282
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:55,120
and you only see the negative side of things

283
00:14:55,120 --> 00:14:56,960
and you focus on the things

284
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:59,920
that are not being done properly in your opinion,

285
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:01,760
that's a really frustrating way to be,

286
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:04,800
And that kind of frustration usually turns into aggression

287
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:07,040
and nobody wants to deal with you. then, okay,

288
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:09,360
I guess what I'm saying

289
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:11,280
is that you want to follow the Scout Oath and Law.

290
00:15:11,280 --> 00:15:12,720
It's not readily apparent

291
00:15:12,720 --> 00:15:14,560
in these sorts of situations sometimes,

292
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:15,440
but it really works.

293
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:17,600
So I think the first thing to do

294
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:19,920
is to look at your own attitude.

295
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:22,160
What do you really want out of this situation?

296
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:24,000
What do you want for your child?

297
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,720
What do you want for the other children who are involved?

298
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:28,480
And what's going to be the best way

299
00:15:28,480 --> 00:15:30,160
of getting to that point?

300
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:31,360
Is it going to be barging in

301
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:34,080
and making a whole bunch of pronouncements and judgments

302
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:36,880
and looking at all the negative things that you perceive?

303
00:15:36,880 --> 00:15:38,000
Obviously not.

304
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,400
It's going to be the Scout Oath and Law-

305
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:44,240
courteous, kind, friendly, obedient, cheerful, loyal-

306
00:15:44,880 --> 00:15:47,680
All of those things- are going to be the kind of person

307
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:50,960
who becomes valuable to the unit

308
00:15:50,960 --> 00:15:52,560
and becomes a trusted advisor

309
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:54,640
of those people who are in key positions.

310
00:15:54,640 --> 00:15:56,800
I know what it's like to have a lot of energy

311
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:58,320
for innovation and change

312
00:15:58,320 --> 00:16:00,080
and to kind of get turned back.

313
00:16:00,080 --> 00:16:02,720
I know what it's like to look at situations

314
00:16:02,720 --> 00:16:06,000
and to think, wow, I could make them better.

315
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,840
I could really help make this better in my own mind.

316
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:14,000
But what you have to do, I think,

317
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:15,600
is take that energy

318
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:19,360
and make sure that you guard it and keep it positive.

319
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:21,200
As I've said a number of times,

320
00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:24,080
that's going to be by building a relationship

321
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:26,400
of mutual respect and trust,

322
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:29,120
And you're going to do that, just like anybody would,

323
00:16:29,120 --> 00:16:32,080
by exercising those principles

324
00:16:32,080 --> 00:16:34,240
that are enshrined in the Scout Oath and Law.

